WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.325
Branches and merging. Work on something new without breaking what already works.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.115
A branch sounds like a copy of your whole project. It isn't.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:18.390
It's a label stuck to one commit. Here, main points at the contact form commit.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:27.360
Run git switch -c add-footer. Git writes one tiny label. Nothing is copied, so it's instant.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:33.013
git branch on its own lists them all. A star marks the one you're on.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.606
HEAD is a you-are-here pin. It now sits on add-footer.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.203
Edit, then commit. The new commit appears, and only the branch you're on moves forward.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.735
Main stays behind. Switch back to it, and the footer is gone.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:58.913
A classic slip: git branch alone makes the label but never moves HEAD. Your commits then land on main.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.431
To bring the footer in, stand on the receiving branch. Switch to main, then git merge add-footer.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:16.270
Main has nothing new, so the history is one straight line. Git just slides the label up. That's a fast-forward.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.035
No new commit. Same history, one label moved.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:28.521
Afterwards, git branch -d add-footer removes the label. The commits stay in main's history.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.654
Now the harder case. Ana starts add-search from the old commit.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.767
While she works, main moves ahead. The history forks.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:49.715
Merge add-search into main. Git finds the common ancestor, combines both sets of changes, and ties the knot with a merge commit.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.501
It has two parents: the tip of main, and the tip of add-search.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.630
The log shows it as a picture: two lines that split, then meet again.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:06.602
One habit. Check which branch you're standing on before you merge.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.286
Merge main into add-footer, and you've updated the wrong branch.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.600
Sometimes Git can't tie the knot. Two branches rewrote the same line: the heading.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:28.193
Everything else, Git still combines by itself. Only that one line needs you.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:35.619
The merge stops with a conflict in index.html. It isn't an error. It's a question.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.862
git status lists the file as both modified.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:47.890
Both versions sit in the file, between markers. Above the ======= is yours. Below is theirs.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:56.697
Edit the line into what you want: yours, theirs, both, or something new. Delete all three markers.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:02.640
Then git add the file, and git commit. The merge is complete.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:12.002
Search for the markers before you commit, or they end up on your page. And if you panic, git merge --abort puts everything back.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.824
Practise it with the Commit and branch simulator, in Version control.
